---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { asset, latestRelease, LATEST_URL, RELEASES_URL, REPO_URL } from "../lib/release";

// Asset names are the ones release.config.mjs and the desktop packaging jobs publish. Renaming
// one there breaks its link here.
const release = await latestRelease();
const published = release?.published.toLocaleDateString("en", { year: "numeric", month: "long", day: "numeric" });
---

<Base title="Download" description="Download MeshClient for the TrimUI Brick, macOS, Windows or Linux.">
    <div class="wrap page">
        <h1>Download</h1>
        {
            release ? (
                <p class="muted">
                    Latest release <a href={release.url}>{release.tag}</a>, published {published}. Every link below
                    points at the newest release.
                </p>
            ) : (
                <p class="muted">
                    Every link below points at the <a href={LATEST_URL}>newest release</a>.
                </p>
            )
        }

        <section class="card">
            <h2>TrimUI Brick</h2>
            <p>
                The easy way is the <a href="https://github.com/LoveRetro/nextui-pak-store">NextUI Pak Store</a>: on
                the Brick, open <strong>Tools → Pak Store</strong>, find <strong>MeshClient</strong> under{" "}
                <strong>Miscellaneous Tools</strong>, and install it.
            </p>
            <p>To install by hand, download the pak and unzip it <em>into</em> a folder you make first:</p>
            <p><a class="button" href={asset("MeshClient.pak.zip")}>MeshClient.pak.zip</a></p>
            <pre><code>mkdir -p /Volumes/SDCARD/Tools/tg5040/MeshClient.pak
unzip MeshClient.pak.zip -d /Volumes/SDCARD/Tools/tg5040/MeshClient.pak</code></pre>
            <p class="muted">
                The zip holds the pak's contents, not the pak folder. Unzipping it without making that folder first
                gives you <code>MeshClient.pak/MeshClient.pak/launch.sh</code>, which the launcher will not run.
            </p>
        </section>

        <p class="note">
            <strong>The desktop builds are experimental.</strong> The Brick is the main target; the desktop builds
            are newer and less tested. Please <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>open an issue</a> when you hit a rough
            edge.
        </p>

        <section class="card">
            <h2>macOS</h2>
            <p>Apple silicon and Intel, macOS 11 or later.</p>
            <p><a class="button" href={asset("MeshClient-macos.dmg")}>MeshClient-macos.dmg</a></p>
            <p>
                Open it and drag <strong>MeshClient</strong> to <strong>Applications</strong>. The app asks for
                Bluetooth the first time it looks for a radio. It updates itself from <strong>Settings → About</strong>{" "}
                once it is in Applications.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card">
            <h2>Windows</h2>
            <p>x64, Windows 10 or later.</p>
            <p>
                <a class="button" href={asset("MeshClient-windows-x86_64-setup.exe")}>
                    MeshClient-windows-x86_64-setup.exe
                </a>
            </p>
            <p>
                It installs for your user only and needs no administrator. The installer is not code-signed yet, so
                SmartScreen may say “Windows protected your PC”: choose <strong>More info → Run anyway</strong>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card">
            <h2>Linux command line</h2>
            <p>One static x86-64 binary with nothing to install: Bluetooth, serial and TCP, all headless.</p>
            <pre><code>curl -LO {asset("meshclient-linux-x86_64")}
curl -LO {asset("meshclient-linux-x86_64.sha256")}
sha256sum -c meshclient-linux-x86_64.sha256
chmod +x meshclient-linux-x86_64 && ./meshclient-linux-x86_64 --status</code></pre>
            <p class="muted">
                On ARM (a Raspberry Pi, say), build it from source with <code>make linux-cli</code>. The{" "}
                <code>meshclient-tg5040-aarch64</code> binary in a release is the handheld's and will not run there.
                Flags and controls are in <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/cli.md`}>docs/cli.md</a>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <p class="muted">
            Older versions and checksums for every file are on the <a href={RELEASES_URL}>releases page</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
</Base>

<style>
    .page {
        padding-top: 3rem;
        max-width: 48rem;
    }
    h1 {
        margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    }
    .card {
        margin-top: 1.5rem;
        background: var(--surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        padding: 0.25rem 1.5rem 0.75rem;
    }
    .card h2 {
        font-size: 1.3rem;
    }
    .note {
        margin-top: 2rem;
        padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
        border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
        background: var(--code-bg);
        border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    }
</style>
